lly, the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. This means that the midpoint of the diagonal connecting \\( a \\) and \\( c \\) is the same as the midpoint of the diagonal connecting \\( b \\) and \\( d \\).\n\nLet's denote the complex number \\( d \\) as \\( d = x + yi \\). The midpoint of the diagonal connecting \\( a \\) and \\( c \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\frac{a + c}{2} = \\frac{(4 + 3i) + (8 - 5i)}{2} = \\frac{12 - 2i}{2} = 6 - i.\n\\]\n\nThe midpoint of the diagonal connecting \\( b \\) and \\( d \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\frac{b + d}{2} = \\frac{(1 - 2i) + (x + yi)}{2} = \\frac{(1 + x) + (y - 2)i}{2} = \\frac{1 + x}{2} + \\frac{y - 2}{2}i.\n\\]\n\nSince these midpoints are the same, we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n6 - i = \\frac{1 + x}{2} + \\frac{y - 2}{2}i.\n\\]\n\nBy comparing the real and imaginary parts, we get two equations:\n\\[\n6 = \\frac{1 + x}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad -1 = \\frac{y - 2}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSolving the first equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n6 = \\frac{1 + x}{2} \\implies 12 = 1 + x \\implies x = 11.\n\\]\n\nSolving the second equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[\n-1 = \\frac{y - 2}{2} \\implies -2 = y - 2 \\implies y = 0.\n\\]\n\nThus, the complex number \\( d \\) is:\n\\[\nd = 11 + 0i = 11.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the possible value of \\( d \\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{11}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:12"}
